  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052415",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-2592",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2feb2115-bd75-5c09-87cc-f10cf46c182f",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Indefinite Integrals of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution",
      "problem": "Find the integral of $\\frac{z+5}{\\sqrt{z+4}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $u=z+4.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}z=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n$z+5=u+1.$\n\nNow we can solve the integral in terms of $u,$ and then express our final answer in terms of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{z+5}{\\sqrt{z+4}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\frac{u+1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{u}{\\sqrt{u}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u+\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u+\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}+2\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{(z+4)^{3}}}{3}+2\\sqrt{z+4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{2\\sqrt[3]{(z+4)^{2}}}{3}+\\sqrt{z+4}+C",
