    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-116551",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d3340444-0db2-5cdd-8032-02955fc2dae0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Asymptotes of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given in Standard Form",
      "problem": "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{6},\\,b=\\sqrt{24}=2\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}}x \\\\  & =\\pm 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
